% =========================================================
% 4. SIGNIFICANCE OF THE PROJECT
% =========================================================
\section{Significance of the Project}

The selected problem is relevant to NLP because YouTube comments represent real-world, noisy and informal textual data.

Unlike formal documents, social media comments may contain:

\begin{itemize}
\item Informal language
\item Spelling variations
\item Abbreviations
\item Emojis
\item Slang
\item Repeated characters
\item URLs
\item User mentions
\item Hindi-English code-mixing
\end{itemize}

Analysing this type of text provides practical experience with several important NLP operations.

The project therefore demonstrates the application of NLP to a realistic social media problem rather than using only clean and formally written text.

% =========================================================
% 5. LITERATURE CONTEXT
% =========================================================
\section{Literature Context}

Sentiment analysis is one of the theoretical research fields in Natural Language Processing. The early approaches were concentrating on revealing whether the document or sentence conveys positive, negative, or neutral messages. Advanced techniques try to specify the emotions being conveyed and the context. \cite{pang,liu}


The social media text causes more additional difficulties, as the users do not adhere to the rules of official writing. For instance, the words may be abbreviated, and distorted, the spelling may be incorrect, and may contain the mix of emotional symbols like emoticons and informal expressions. \cite{bird,barman}


One more significant issue is code-mixing. In cases of multiparty societies, the users can switch between languages when they communicate. The example of code-mixing could be the combination of Hindi and English languages in one unit. \cite{barman}


Therefore, traditional methods of the English language processing will have to undergo additional processing application so as to work with Hinglish comments. \cite{barman}

