\section{Data Preprocessing}

Comments made on social media platforms are characterized by high levels of noise. Sentiment or emotion analysis cannot be performed until the comments are transformed into a clearer and more consistent form. 

The preprocessing pipeline is made up of the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]

\item Data loading
\item Missing-value handling
\item Duplicate removal
\item URL handling
\item Special-character processing
\item Text normalization
\item Lowercase conversion where appropriate
\item Tokenization
\item Stop-word processing
\item Whitespace normalization
\item Feature preparation

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Data Cleaning}

Inspecting the data set is the initial action. Missing textual values are treated in the correct way, while any duplicated observations are eliminated to avoid giving undue weight to repeated comments in the analysis.

\subsection{Handling URLs and Special Characters}

A YouTube comment can have links, mentions, hashtags (abbreviated or un-abbreviated), and special characters. These items can act as noise for the classical text classification system.

Yet, any emotion-laden items (like emojis), should be studied thoroughly, as they can convey relevant emotions. \cite{mohammad}

\subsection{Text Normalization}

Normalization reduces unnecessary variation in textual representation. \cite{jurafsky,bird}

For example:

\begin{quote}
``THIS VIDEO IS AMAZING!!!''
\end{quote}

can be normalized to:

\begin{quote}
``this video is amazing''
\end{quote}

In the case of Hinglish comments, it is crucial that normalization retains Hindi words written in the Roman script, as they hold significance to the current issue being dealt with.

